## 5. Modular design shape

The execute-action namespace is intentionally split into small modules:

```text
capabilityBusExecuteActionCommon.mjs       shared contract version/copy/prefix helpers
capabilityBusExecuteActionPlan.mjs         action + registries -> accepted execute-action descriptor validation
capabilityBusExecuteActionResult.mjs       accepted result/event and validation-failed result metadata helpers
capabilityBusExecuteActionContract.mjs     thin public barrel/export surface
```

The namespace lives under `runtime/bus/executeAction/` because this is a bus/action boundary, not a backend execution implementation.

`runtime/execution/` remains the metadata-only execution descriptor namespace and was not broadened into a god executor surface.

---

## 6. Contract behavior

The new contract exposes:

```js
validateCapabilityBusExecuteActionPlan(actionEnvelope, registries)
normalizeCapabilityBusExecuteActionPlan(actionEnvelope, registries)
assertCapabilityBusExecuteActionPlan(actionEnvelope, registries)
createCapabilityBusExecuteActionAcceptedResult(plan, details)
createCapabilityBusExecuteActionAcceptedEvent(plan, fields)
createCapabilityBusExecuteActionValidationFailedResult(actionEnvelope, validationResult, details)
```

Expected registry input shape:

```js
const registries = {
    capabilityRegistry,
    routerRegistry,
    serviceRegistry,
    backendAdapterRegistry
};
```

Accepted plan shape:

```js
const plan = {
    contractVersion: "capability-bus-execute-action.v1",
    status: "accepted",
    action: {
        actionId,
        runId,
        capability
    },
    busAction,
    routePlan,
    servicePlan,
    backendPlan,
    executionPlan
};
```

Accepted result/event helpers produce metadata only:

```text
status: accepted
event type: action.accepted
```

They do not claim execution started, streamed, completed, failed, or touched a backend.

